window.CHAR_BIOS = window.CHAR_BIOS || {};

window.CHAR_BIOS['malfurion'] = [
  ["A Student of Cenarius", [
    "Malfurion Stormrage and his twin brother Illidan were born at Lorlathil in Val'sharah and grew up in the city of Suramar. Their parents died years before the war that made them famous, and their closest friend was a girl named Tyrande Whisperwind. Malfurion loved the natural world at a time when most night elves cared only for the arcane power of the Well of Eternity. Following old legends, he went looking for the forest demigod Cenarius, who had withdrawn from the night elves long before. Cenarius found the young elf first, saw great promise in him, and offered to teach him. Malfurion became the first mortal druid on Azeroth.",
    "Tyrande already served the moon goddess Elune, and Illidan lacked the patience for druidism, so Malfurion trained alone in the forests of Val'sharah. Both brothers loved Tyrande. She chose Malfurion, and that choice shaped all three of their lives."
  ]],
  ["War Against the Queen", [
    "When Queen Azshara's Highborne opened the way for the Burning Legion, Malfurion joined the resistance under Lord Kur'talos Ravencrest. His first great blow came through the Emerald Dream, a spirit realm that mirrors the waking world. He sent his spirit through it into Azshara's palace, broke the spell that sustained the demons' portal, and killed the queen's counselor Xavius. He also urged Cenarius to rally the Wild Gods, and he went in search of the dragons.",
    "As the war dragged on, Malfurion became certain that the Well of Eternity itself had to be destroyed. His companions were horrified, since the Well fed their magic, but Tyrande helped persuade them. Illidan could not accept it and went over to the Legion. In the final battle at Zin-Azshari, Malfurion drew on the Dragon Soul and called down a storm that tore apart the Highborne's spellwork. The Well collapsed, and the Great Sundering followed.",
    "On Mount Hyjal, Malfurion found that Illidan had poured stolen water from the old Well into a lake and made a new one. He could not bring himself to have his brother killed. With Cenarius's help he sealed Illidan in a prison beneath the mountain, guarded by Maiev Shadowsong. Over the following ages he sometimes visited the cell, hoping to turn his twin from his path."
  ]],
  ["The Long Sleep", [
    "Malfurion asked the dragons to guard the new Well, and they planted the World Tree Nordrassil over it. To help the green dragon Ysera tend the Emerald Dream, the druids agreed to sleep in it for centuries at a time. Malfurion spent most of the next ten thousand years in a barrow den in Moonglade while Tyrande led their people. He woke when he was needed. He decided that the Highborne who defied the ban on magic should be exiled, and when some of his druids took on a wolf form he had forbidden and became the first worgen, he founded the Cenarion Circle and sealed them away in the Dream with the Scythe of Elune.",
    "Tyrande woke him with the Horn of Cenarius when the Legion returned in the Third War. He objected when she freed Illidan, and he banished his brother after Illidan absorbed the Skull of Gul'dan and took on a demon's shape. At the prophet Medivh's urging he joined forces with the humans and orcs. At the Battle of Mount Hyjal he sounded the horn, and the spirits of the night elves' ancestors poured their strength into Nordrassil and destroyed the demon lord Archimonde. The night elves lost their immortality in the blast. Months later, Malfurion and Illidan rescued Tyrande together in Lordaeron, and Malfurion let his brother go."
  ]],
  ["Lost in the Dream", [
    "Soon after the war, Malfurion's spirit went into the Dream and did not come back. His body lay asleep in Moonglade for years. In his absence Archdruid Fandral Staghelm planted a new World Tree, Teldrassil, which Malfurion had warned against. The truth came out later. Xavius had survived as the Nightmare Lord, a servant of the Old God N'Zoth, and he held Malfurion captive in the corrupted part of the Dream while Fandral kept Malfurion's body weak.",
    "After the Lich King's fall, Tyrande and the druid Broll Bearmantle went into the Dream, and the orc Thura cut Malfurion free. Malfurion exposed Fandral, cleansed Teldrassil, and traveled in spirit to rally the leaders of the world against the Nightmare. He killed Xavius again. He and Tyrande then married in Darnassus and ruled the night elves together. During the Cataclysm he held the land of Darkshore together against raging elementals and led the druids against the Firelord Ragnaros on Mount Hyjal. Maiev, enraged that he had welcomed the Highborne back into night elf society, tried to murder him, and her brother Jarod saved him."
  ]],
  ["Fire, Sacrifice and Rest", [
    "In the Legion's last invasion, Xavius returned in Val'sharah, corrupted Cenarius and captured Malfurion. Ysera died in the struggle, and Malfurion later helped cleanse his old teacher. Before the final battle on Argus, Illidan left him a message asking to end their long strife and to take care of Tyrande. In his reply, Malfurion said his brother's crimes were hard to forgive, yet he remembered the days they had fought side by side as good ones.",
    "When the Horde invaded Ashenvale in the War of the Thorns, Malfurion held the forest almost single-handed, ambushing the invaders and walling Darkshore off with wisps. He fought Sylvanas Windrunner to a standstill until Varok Saurfang struck him from behind. Saurfang refused to finish him, and Tyrande arrived in time to heal him. Teldrassil burned soon after. Malfurion later fought beside Tyrande when she returned to Darkshore as the Night Warrior.",
    "During Dragonflight, Malfurion gave himself up so that Ysera could live again, taking her place in Ardenweald, the realm of the dead she had been bound to. He slept there while the new World Tree, Amirdrassil, grew and bloomed on the Dragon Isles. When he returned, he and Tyrande named their adopted daughter Shandris Feathermoon leader of the night elves and stepped down to spend time together in Bel'ameth. He lives there still as an elder archdruid. In Midnight it was Shandris who led the night elves to war for the Sunwell.",
    "Across his long life Malfurion has been patient and merciful, sometimes to his own cost. He has said he wants only a simple life. He has rarely been allowed one."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['tyrande'] = [
  ["Priestess of Suramar", [
    "Tyrande Whisperwind was born in Suramar and grew up beside the twins Malfurion and Illidan Stormrage, often beating both of them at their games. She entered the Temple of Elune as a novice priestess while Malfurion studied with Cenarius and Illidan turned to sorcery. Both brothers loved her. She chose Malfurion, and Illidan never let go of that wound.",
    "When the Burning Legion came, the Sisterhood of Elune turned their temple into a refuge for the wounded and then took up arms beside Lord Kur'talos Ravencrest's army. During the war Tyrande was named High Priestess, though she was young and new to the order. She also took in Shandris Feathermoon, a girl who had lost her family to the demons, and raised her as a daughter. Tyrande saw that Malfurion was right about destroying the Well of Eternity and helped convince the others, and she survived the Great Sundering that followed."
  ]],
  ["Ten Thousand Years Awake", [
    "After the Sundering, Tyrande led the survivors and reshaped their society. She organized the Sentinels, an army made up mostly of women and directed by the Sisterhood, and she and Malfurion exiled the Highborne who would not give up arcane magic. Malfurion and his druids then went to sleep in the Emerald Dream. Tyrande begged him to stay, and he went anyway. For ten thousand years she guarded Kalimdor with only brief visits from him, and she grew harder and fiercer in that time. When she woke him during the Third War, she told him sharply that she had not had the luxury of sleeping through times of peril.",
    "In that war she fought the orcs who had killed Cenarius. She had the Watchers who refused her struck down so she could free Illidan to fight the demons, and then watched in horror as he became half demon. At Medivh's urging she accepted an alliance with the humans and orcs, and the night elves held Mount Hyjal until Archimonde was destroyed. Later, chasing Illidan into Lordaeron with Maiev Shadowsong, she was swept down a river while holding a bridge against the Scourge. Illidan himself fought through the undead to rescue her."
  ]],
  ["Ruler of Darnassus", [
    "With Malfurion lost in the Dream after the war, Tyrande ruled the night elves alone from Darnassus, the city in the branches of the new World Tree Teldrassil, and brought her people into the Alliance. She quarreled often with Archdruid Fandral Staghelm. When visions warned her that Malfurion was dying, she went into the Dream herself to find him. After the Nightmare was defeated, the two finally married in Darnassus, and she insisted that he share the rule with her.",
    "In the years that followed she welcomed the worgen of Gilneas, fought Garrosh Hellscream's Horde for Ashenvale, and pledged her people to King Varian Wrynn's command in Pandaria. At Garrosh's trial she served as the accuser, and her hatred often showed. Tyrande fears becoming another Azshara, and she has asked people not to praise her with grand titles.",
    "In Legion, the Nightmare Lord Xavius captured Malfurion and made Tyrande choose between following her husband and defending Elune's temple in Val'sharah. She chose the temple. She later led the Sentinels to Suramar, the city of her birth, to help free it from the Legion. She distrusted its nightborne, who had hidden behind their shield while the rest of Kalimdor burned ten thousand years before."
  ]],
  ["The Night Warrior", [
    "In the War of the Thorns, the Horde invaded Ashenvale while Tyrande was in Stormwind. She came back in time to save Malfurion from death and spared Varok Saurfang because he had refused to kill her husband. Soon after, the Horde burned Teldrassil with its people inside. Tyrande named the last baby carried out alive Finel, which means the last, and blamed herself for showing Saurfang mercy.",
    "She went ahead alone to Darkshore and performed the ritual of the Night Warrior, the aspect of Elune's wrath, which had torn apart everyone who tried it in living memory. She told Elune she would go on serving her only if the goddess granted her justice. Tyrande survived and became the avatar of that wrath. With Maiev and Shandris she retook Darkshore. She refused to sign the peace that ended the war, and she later killed Sylvanas Windrunner's champion, Nathanos Blightcaller.",
    "Tyrande followed Sylvanas into the Maw, the prison of the Shadowlands, where many night elf souls lost in the fire were trapped. The Night Warrior's power was killing her. When she tried to spend her life to destroy Sylvanas, Elune withdrew her power and Sylvanas escaped. In a ritual in Ardenweald, Elune spoke through Tyrande and left the choice to her. Tyrande chose renewal over vengeance. After the Jailer's defeat, a remorseful Sylvanas gave herself up to Tyrande's judgment, and Tyrande sentenced her to free every soul trapped in the Maw."
  ]],
  ["A New Tree", [
    "The Winter Queen of Ardenweald gave Tyrande the Sisters' Tear, a seed born from Elune's tear and the rescued souls of night elves. In Dragonflight, Tyrande planted it in the Emerald Dream. To bring Ysera back to life, Malfurion took the dragon's place in Ardenweald, and Tyrande let him go, though she was weary of being left alone. She led the night elves in defending the young tree from the fire dragon Fyrakk and the Druids of the Flame. When Amirdrassil bloomed on the Dragon Isles, she made its first moonwell from the last water saved from Darnassus and named the new city Bel'ameth, the Arms of the Goddess.",
    "When Malfurion returned, the two named Shandris leader of the night elves and stepped down. Tyrande remains High Priestess of Elune and lives with her husband in Bel'ameth. She is caring and devout, and she can also be rash. When her people are harmed, her anger can be terrible."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['illidan'] = [
  ["The Twin With Golden Eyes", [
    "Illidan Stormrage was born at Lorlathil in Val'sharah, the twin of Malfurion. Unlike his brother, he had golden eyes, which night elves of that age read as a sign of a great destiny. The twins grew up in Suramar with their friend Tyrande Whisperwind. Illidan tried to learn druidism from Cenarius alongside Malfurion, but he was clumsy and impatient with it, and the arcane called to him instead. He became a gifted sorcerer, though he was never one of the Highborne nobility.",
    "Illidan loved Tyrande and showed off his magic to impress her. She chose Malfurion. That loss, his hunger for magic and his rivalry with his brother drove much of what he did for the next ten thousand years."
  ]],
  ["Betrayer at the Well", [
    "When the Burning Legion invaded, Illidan became the personal sorcerer of Lord Kur'talos Ravencrest and later led the Moon Guard, an order of night elf mages. He drained his mages' power to strengthen his own spells, sometimes until they died, and saw this as necessary sacrifice. When Malfurion decided the Well of Eternity had to be destroyed, Illidan could not accept losing the magic it gave him. He went to Zin-Azshari and offered himself to the Legion.",
    "Sargeras, still beyond the portal, burned out Illidan's eyes and gave him a sight that could see every kind of magic, and fel tattoos spread across his skin. Current lore says Illidan meant his betrayal at first, but the vision Sargeras showed him of the Legion's endless war across countless worlds changed him, and he resolved to find a way to destroy the Legion for good. He stole the Dragon Soul for the Highborne, then rejoined his brother and claimed it had all been a ruse. Before the Well collapsed, he quietly filled vials with its water.",
    "On Mount Hyjal he poured some of that water into a lake and created a new Well, arguing that his people would need magic if the Legion returned. The other night elves saw only a new disaster. Malfurion had him sealed in a prison beneath the mountain, and Maiev Shadowsong became his jailer. Older accounts told this story differently, and his motives have been rewritten more than once."
  ]],
  ["Freed and Banished", [
    "Illidan spent ten thousand years in the dark. In the Third War, Tyrande freed him to fight the demons. In the forest of Felwood he met the death knight Arthas, who told him about the Skull of Gul'dan. Illidan took the skull's power, destroyed the dreadlord Tichondrius, and became half demon. Malfurion banished him.",
    "The demon lord Kil'jaeden then offered Illidan power if he destroyed the Lich King's Frozen Throne. Illidan called up the naga under Lady Vashj and took the Eye of Sargeras from the Tomb of Sargeras, bringing the tomb down on Maiev's Watchers. When Tyrande came after him, she finally told him why she had chosen Malfurion. Raw power, she said, could never replace inner strength. His spell to break the ice of Northrend failed when Malfurion and Maiev stopped him. After he saved Tyrande from the Scourge, Malfurion let him go, and Illidan fled to Outland, the shattered remains of the orc homeworld. There Prince Kael'thas Sunstrider's blood elves joined him, Akama of the Broken draenei pledged his people, and together they overthrew the pit lord Magtheridon. Illidan then marched on Icecrown, but Arthas beat him in single combat and became the new Lich King."
  ]],
  ["Lord of Outland", [
    "Illidan ruled Outland from the Black Temple. He bred fel orcs from Magtheridon's blood and trained elves as demon hunters, the Illidari, who took demonic power into themselves to fight demons. He destroyed the dreadlord world of Nathreza, took the location of Argus, the Legion's homeworld, and prepared to strike it. The Legion learned of his plans, and Kil'jaeden lured the armies of Azeroth into Outland so that they and Illidan would wear each other down. Illidan's single-minded focus also drove away his allies. Kael'thas turned to Kil'jaeden, and Akama freed Maiev and helped heroes from Azeroth storm the temple. Illidan died there in the Burning Crusade, whispering Tyrande's name. Maiev locked his body in the Vault of the Wardens."
  ]],
  ["The Hunter Returns", [
    "In Legion, Gul'dan stole Illidan's body to make it a vessel for Sargeras. The naaru Xe'ra, leader of the Army of the Light, helped the Illidari recover his soul, and in the Nighthold he woke in his own body and killed Gul'dan. Illidan fought beside Azeroth's armies, trading barbs with Maiev as they retook the Broken Shore, and after Kil'jaeden's death he pulled Argus into Azeroth's sky so the war could be carried there.",
    "Xe'ra believed Illidan was the Child of Light and Shadow, a hero marked from his birth to end the age of demons. On Argus she bound him in chains of Light to force that destiny on him. Illidan broke free, declared his fate his own, and destroyed her. After the final battle, the titan Pantheon imprisoned Sargeras at their Seat, and Illidan chose to stay behind as his jailer, saying the hunter is nothing without the hunt.",
    "Before that battle he left messages for his brother and for Tyrande. He told Malfurion that he had sought power only to save their world and wished to end their long strife. He told Tyrande she had been his anchor and that her heart had chosen rightly. As of Midnight he is not known to have left his post over Sargeras.",
    "Illidan was arrogant and ruthless with other lives, his own soldiers' included, and his methods earned him the name Betrayer. In his later years his goal never changed. He meant to end the Legion, whatever it cost."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['azshara'] = [
  ["Light of Lights", [
    "Azshara was born with golden eyes, a rare mark that the ancient night elves read as a sign of future greatness. She was one of the Highborne and the sole heir to the throne, and she became the most beloved ruler in night elf history. Her coronation lasted for days. Of all the gifts, she treasured a jeweled scepter from the noble Xavius, who promised it would bring her power. She later infused it with water from the Well of Eternity and named it Sharas'dal, the Scepter of Tides.",
    "Azshara was also one of the greatest sorcerers who ever lived, though she hid how strong she was. Her people renamed their capital Zin-Azshari, the Glory of Azshara, and loved her so much that their love came close to worship. In public she was gracious and generous. In private she cared for no one but herself. She favored the Highborne only because they were useful to her, and she secretly loathed Mount Hyjal and the wild gods who lived there. Under her rule the empire reached its height, founding distant outposts and raising a great temple to Elune."
  ]],
  ["The Queen and the Dark Titan", [
    "Xavius, now her chief counselor, was the first to hear the voice of Sargeras, the fallen titan who leads the Burning Legion. He brought it to Azshara. Sargeras promised to help her remake the world, and Azshara pictured a Kalimdor cleansed of everyone she thought impure, including the ordinary night elves who adored her. She came to believe Sargeras was the only being worthy to rule beside her.",
    "At her command the Highborne opened a portal in the Well of Eternity, and demons poured out of her palace. Most night elves refused to believe their queen was to blame and told themselves she was a prisoner. Azshara let the Legion slaughter her own people. When Prince Farondis of Azsuna plotted to destroy the Well, she destroyed his titan relic and cursed his land, and its people still wander there as ghosts. She had the resistance leader, Lord Kur'talos Ravencrest, assassinated. Even the demon lord Mannoroth, enraged by her questions, held back when he sensed that only Archimonde and Sargeras could match her."
  ]],
  ["Bargain in the Deep", [
    "The resistance shattered the Highborne's spellwork, and the Well collapsed in the Great Sundering. Azshara knew Sargeras would not come, and she did not tell her followers. She raised a shield over herself and her court, but the sea broke through. As she sank, the Old God N'Zoth spoke to her through a small fish with red eyes and offered to save her in exchange for service. Azshara refused to be a servant. She insisted she would rule at his side as a queen, and N'Zoth reluctantly agreed. He changed her and her Highborne into the naga, and Azshara herself swelled into a huge, many-armed monster.",
    "For ten thousand years she built a new empire beneath the sea, with its capital at Nazjatar. Her sea witches wielded Sharas'dal against sea giants and raiders. Even beneath the waves she kept her vanity. Her throne room was a sealed chamber of air, decorated with fountains and waterfalls. When the Old Gods wished it, her handmaiden Lady Vashj led naga to serve Illidan Stormrage. Those on the surface who remembered Azshara believed she was dead."
  ]],
  ["The Long Game", [
    "Azshara returned to the surface world step by step. During the Cataclysm she stirred up trouble in Darkshore to keep Malfurion Stormrage away from Mount Hyjal, and she worked to destroy Neptulon, the elemental lord of water. In Legion her naga hunted the Tidestone of Golganneth in Azsuna, and she appeared again before Farondis to promise her wrath. In Battle for Azeroth she corrupted the tidesages of Kul Tiras, made a pact with the Zandalari traitor Zul, and armed her naga with azerite weapons made by Priscilla Ashvane, head of a Kul Tiran trading company.",
    "Her greatest move came after the Battle of Dazar'alor. As the Horde and Alliance fleets sailed above Nazjatar, she used the Tidestone to split the ocean and dropped both fleets onto the seafloor. She welcomed them to her realm, toyed with them, and invited them into her Eternal Palace. Every step had been planned. Azshara drew on the power the champions had built up in the Heart of Azeroth, an amulet filled by the world-soul, to break the last seals on N'Zoth's prison. When she fell, the final seal broke, and a tentacle of the Old God carried her into the deep."
  ]],
  ["Escape", [
    "In N'Zoth's sunken city of Ny'alotha, his servants tortured her. Freed by the black dragon Wrathion and a band of champions, she handed them Xal'atath, a whispering Old God dagger, and warned that if they failed to kill N'Zoth with their first blow they would not get another. Then she declared she was tired of serving others and would claim the true throne of power for herself. She opened a Void portal and vanished.",
    "Her whereabouts have been unknown ever since. A naga text found later claims she calls to her followers from beyond something it names the Umbral Veil and will return to rule sea, sky and earth. Without her, the naga of Nazjatar have split into feuding factions. During The War Within she sent the Darktide naga to seize a singing crystal on the newly found Siren Isle, so she is still at work somewhere. As of Midnight, no one knows where she is or what she plans.",
    "Azshara has always been charming, vain and utterly selfish. She sacrificed her people twice, first to the Legion and then to N'Zoth, and she has never truly belonged to any master."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['kaelthas'] = [
  ["Prince of the Kirin Tor", [
    "Kael'thas Sunstrider was the only son of King Anasterian of Quel'Thalas. He spent most of his long life in Dalaran, where he rose to the Council of Six that led the Kirin Tor mages. Unlike most high elves, he wanted to see the world and meet other peoples, and his father worried that his loyalty was split between Dalaran and home. In Dalaran he courted the young sorceress Jaina Proudmoore, who chose Prince Arthas Menethil instead.",
    "When Arthas led the Scourge through Quel'Thalas, killed Anasterian and corrupted the Sunwell, Kael'thas lashed out at Jaina and went home to a cold welcome. He led volunteers to destroy the tainted Sunwell, renamed the survivors sin'dorei, children of the blood, and decreed that his father would be the last king of Quel'Thalas. Leaving Lor'themar Theron as regent, he took a band of elves to fight the Scourge with what was left of the Alliance."
  ]],
  ["Into Outland", [
    "The Alliance commander, Grand Marshal Garithos, despised elves. When Kael'thas accepted help from Lady Vashj's naga, Garithos jailed him and his people for treason. Vashj freed them and led them to Outland, where they rescued Illidan Stormrage from Maiev Shadowsong. Illidan promised to feed the elves' hunger for magic, and Kael'thas pledged his people to him. At Icecrown he dueled Arthas and was forced to retreat.",
    "In Outland, Kael'thas grew desperate. He had found no cure for his people's addiction, fel magic was eating at his mind, and he came to believe Illidan saw the blood elves only as tools. Some of his finest magisters deserted him. Kil'jaeden offered him power and salvation for his people, and Kael'thas accepted. He seized Tempest Keep, a fortress of the naaru, and sent the captured naaru M'uru to Silvermoon to be drained. He kept up the image of a devoted prince until defectors called the Scryers exposed his pact with the Legion, and the Horde declared war on him."
  ]],
  ["The Sun King Falls", [
    "Heroes defeated Kael'thas in Tempest Keep, but his demon allies saved him. He returned to Quel'Thalas twisted by fel, with a crystal lodged in his chest, and seized M'uru and Anveena, the mortal avatar of the Sunwell's power. He meant to turn the Sunwell into a gateway for Kil'jaeden. Heroes killed him in Magisters' Terrace in the Burning Crusade, and Kil'jaeden was driven back. His scheme led indirectly to the Sunwell's rebirth, when the prophet Velen used M'uru's heart to cleanse it.",
    "His soul went to Revendreth in the Shadowlands, a realm where proud souls atone. Sire Denathrius tortured him there and loaded him with the sins of others. The Accuser later took over his redemption. Freed of those extra sins, Kael'thas began to accept his own guilt, and the Accuser told him that failing to prevent Quel'Thalas's fall was not one of his crimes. He agreed to forgive himself for it and went on serving the venthyr. In the rebuilt Silvermoon of Midnight, no statue of him still stands.",
    "Kael'thas loved his people and his homeland. He was also proud, and the lengths he went to for them destroyed most of who he was."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['lorthemar'] = [
  ["Ranger of Silvermoon", [
    "Lor'themar Theron spent most of his life as a Farstrider, one of the rangers of Quel'Thalas. As a young lieutenant he was captured and tortured by the Amani troll warlord Zul'jin, along with the priestess Liadrin. He served under Ranger-General Lireesa Windrunner and became close friends with her daughter Sylvanas and the ranger Halduron Brightwing. When Sylvanas became ranger-general, Lor'themar was her second-in-command, and he fought in the Second War beside Alleria Windrunner and the human paladin Turalyon.",
    "During the Scourge invasion he found the kingdom's magical defenses betrayed and was nearly killed by risen dead, who scarred his face and blinded his left eye. He survived the fall of Silvermoon, gathered survivors, and helped Prince Kael'thas destroy the corrupted Sunwell. Kael'thas left him as regent when he went to fight the Scourge and later to Outland."
  ]],
  ["The Reluctant Regent", [
    "Lor'themar rebuilt Silvermoon with Grand Magister Rommath and exiled elves who refused Rommath's new way of draining magic, a choice that weighed on him. With few allies left, he accepted Sylvanas's help and brought the blood elves into the Horde. When Kael'thas betrayed his people to the Legion, Lor'themar agreed he had to be stopped. After the prince died, Lor'themar refused the crown and kept the title of regent lord, honoring the decree that Anasterian would be the last king.",
    "He grew into a careful politician who never stopped missing a ranger's life. He also helped raise Alleria's son Arator in Silvermoon and trained him in combat. When Warchief Garrosh Hellscream treated his people as suspects, he opened talks with the Alliance, until Jaina Proudmoore's purge of the blood elves in Dalaran pushed him back. He then backed Vol'jin's rebellion. In Battle for Azeroth he turned Alleria away when she asked Quel'Thalas to rejoin the Alliance, and he banished her after her Void powers drew monsters to the Sunwell. He sided with Saurfang against Sylvanas, joined the Horde Council, and married Thalyssra, leader of the nightborne, with Lady Liadrin presiding."
  ]],
  ["Defender of the Sunwell", [
    "In Midnight, Xal'atath's Devouring Host attacked the Sunwell. Lor'themar held the walls while Liadrin prayed, and Xal'atath's mental assault brought him to his knees before the Light answered with reinforcements. He clashed again and again with Turalyon, who led those forces. Lor'themar insisted that saving civilians came first and reminded the paladin that Silvermoon belonged to the blood elves. He also led his army against the Amani trolls before learning that both peoples faced a common enemy in the Twilight's Blade cult.",
    "After Xal'atath corrupted the Sunwell into the Darkwell, Lor'themar went with Thalyssra and Magister Umbric to ask the night elves for help, and he fought on the bridge to Quel'Danas. The cleansed well became the Dawnwell. When the children of the serpent Ula'tek were loosed on Zul'Aman, he refused to send troops, judging it not Silvermoon's problem. He regretted it when the Twilight's Blade moved to free Kith'ix, an ancient general of the Old Gods whom Ula'tek had kept sealed away. He also shared a drink with an old Amani foe, the general Torundo, and the two came away with some understanding. As of Midnight he still rules as regent lord. He can be stubborn and proud, and he puts the safety of his people above everything else."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['alleria'] = [
  ["The Eldest Windrunner", [
    "Alleria Windrunner was the eldest child of Lireesa Windrunner, Ranger-General of Silvermoon. She was raised to succeed her mother, but she chafed at the high elves' isolation and wanted to see the world. She gave up her claim, put forward her sister Sylvanas as heir, and left Quel'Thalas with her own company of rangers.",
    "In the Second War, Alleria led her rangers to the Alliance without orders, convinced the Horde threatened every kingdom. She fought beside the paladin Turalyon and the mage Khadgar. When the Horde burned Eversong, her younger brother Lirath was among the dead. Alleria fell in love with Turalyon and bore him a son, Arator, but grief drove her into a long hunt for orcs. When the Alliance sent an expedition through the Dark Portal to Draenor, she left Arator with her sister Vereesa and went. As Draenor tore itself apart, she and Turalyon escaped through a rift into the Twisting Nether, and Azeroth believed them dead."
  ]],
  ["A Thousand Years of War", [
    "In the Nether they joined the Army of the Light under the naaru Xe'ra. Time ran differently there, and they fought the Legion for what felt to them like a thousand years. Alleria was touched by the Void, the dark force opposed to the Light, and learned to command it from Locus-Walker, an ethereal who had mastered it. Xe'ra imprisoned her for refusing to give it up. On Argus she absorbed the essence of the fallen naaru L'ura and became the first void elf, insisting she was still herself.",
    "Back on Azeroth, a reunion with her sisters at Windrunner Spire ended in bitterness. Her Void power then drew monsters to the Sunwell, and Lor'themar Theron banished her from Quel'Thalas. She tracked down Magister Umbric's exiles, saved them from being consumed as they turned into void elves, and became their leader in the Alliance."
  ]],
  ["Hunting the Harbinger", [
    "Alleria spent the following years hunting Xal'atath, a Void being known as the Harbinger. She watched her destroy Dalaran, cracked her relic the Dark Heart with an arrow in Hallowfall, and saw her kill Locus-Walker on K'aresh. Alleria swore an oath there to avenge him. In the same years Turalyon proposed and she accepted, but after a demon attack broke up the wedding she called it off, admitting that she now feared his Light."
  ]],
  ["Lost at the Voidspire", [
    "In Midnight, Alleria was stranded in the Voidstorm, the dark realm where Xal'atath built her stronghold. She fought there with Arator and the dreadlord paladin Lothraxion, forced the domanaar Decimus to serve them, and loosed L'ura to destroy a naaru that the Shadowguard had turned to the Void. When she learned that Turalyon's Light-driven fury had wounded their son, she confronted him and begged him not to lose himself.",
    "At the Voidspire, Xal'atath captured her, broke her control and tore L'ura out of her body to corrupt the Sunwell. Alleria and Turalyon were lost to the Void, and their fate is unknown. Alleria has always chosen the wider fight over home and paid for it with time away from her family. By her own account, she did it to keep the people she loves safe."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['anasterian'] = [
  ["The Old King", [
    "Anasterian Sunstrider, great-grandson of the founder Dath'Remar, ruled Quel'Thalas for at least 2,800 years. He came to power during the Troll Wars, when the Amani trolls nearly destroyed his kingdom. He asked the young human nation of Arathor for help and sent elven mages to teach a hundred humans magic. Together they broke the trolls, and the elves swore loyalty to the bloodline of King Thoradin.",
    "That oath drew him into the Second War, but he sent only a token force until the Horde and the Amani set Eversong ablaze. When orcs killed his advisers Lireesa and Verath Windrunner, he burned the arrow that proved the Horde's guilt and forbade revenge, to keep his kingdom out of the war. After the war he declared the debt paid, blamed the humans for the burned forests, and took Quel'Thalas out of the Alliance."
  ]],
  ["Death on Quel'Danas", [
    "In the Third War, Arthas led the Scourge to Silvermoon. Anasterian refused to surrender, retreated to the Isle of Quel'Danas, and faced Arthas in single combat. Old and frail, he still crippled Arthas's horse before Frostmourne shattered his family's runeblade, Felo'melorn, and killed him. Arthas then used the Sunwell to raise Kel'Thuzad. Anasterian's only son, Kael'thas, decreed that no one would be king after him.",
    "Anasterian was proud, cautious and often slow to decide, and he believed that staying out of the world's troubles would keep his people safe. Opinions of him remain divided. Blood elves still charge into battle shouting his name. In Midnight, the Silver Covenant returned relics of his reign to Silvermoon as an act of atonement, and Vereesa Windrunner and Aethas Sunreaver offered his crown to help cleanse the Darkwell into the Dawnwell, as a farewell to the last king."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['liadrin'] = [
  ["Priestess Without Faith", [
    "Liadrin lost her parents to Amani trolls and was raised by High Priest Vandellor, whose rank she later reached. As a young priestess she was captured by the troll warlord Zul'jin along with Lor'themar Theron and helped the prisoners escape. When the Scourge took Quel'Thalas, she could not call on the Light as her kingdom fell. Vandellor was killed and rose as one of the undead, and she had to strike him down herself. Blaming the Light, she put down her priestly vows and spent years destroying undead in the Ghostlands."
  ]],
  ["The First Blood Knight", [
    "Grand Magister Rommath offered her a new path. Prince Kael'thas had sent the captured naaru M'uru to Silvermoon, and Liadrin drew the Light from it by force, becoming the first of the Blood Knights. When Kael'thas betrayed his people, she renounced House Sunstrider and fought with the Shattered Sun Offensive. She stood with the prophet Velen when he used M'uru's heart to cleanse the Sunwell, and the Sunwell's rebirth restored her faith. She and her order then turned to the Light freely.",
    "She helped bring the nightborne into the Horde and presided at Lor'themar's wedding to Thalyssra. In Midnight, as Xal'atath's army reached the Sunwell, Liadrin prayed in its waters, feeling unworthy herself but certain her people were worth saving. The Light answered with the Vanguard of the Light. She later earned the respect of the Amani by helping their new leader Zul'jarra win back their loa, the trolls' animal gods. After the Darkwell was broken, she led the ritual, filled the cleansed well with Light and named it the Dawnwell. She remains matriarch of the Blood Knights."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['vereesa'] = [
  ["Youngest of the Windrunners", [
    "Vereesa was the youngest daughter of Lireesa and Verath Windrunner. She secretly trained her little brother Lirath to fight, and when he died in the Second War, Sylvanas blamed her, opening a long rift between them. As a young ranger she escorted the human wizard Rhonin and helped him free the red Dragonqueen Alexstrasza from the orcs at Grim Batol. She married Rhonin, raised her sister Alleria's son Arator, and bore twin sons, Giramar and Galadin. Rhonin's support helped her through the elves' hunger for magic, and she remained a high elf."
  ]],
  ["From Grief to the Silversun", [
    "In Dalaran she founded the Silver Covenant, high elves who opposed letting blood elves into the Kirin Tor. After Garrosh Hellscream's mana bomb killed Rhonin at Theramore, she helped Jaina Proudmoore purge the Sunreaver blood elves from Dalaran. At Garrosh's trial she plotted with Sylvanas to poison him, then backed out for her sons' sake and told Anduin Wrynn. A later reunion of the three sisters at Windrunner Spire went badly.",
    "Visions of Silvermoon falling to the Void brought her home in Midnight. Haunted by the Sunreavers slain in the purge, she carried relics of King Anasterian's reign and the ashes of the dead to Silvermoon as an act of atonement. She fought with Arator against the dark naaru L'ura and embraced Sylvanas when her sister stepped out of the Maw. Afterward she disbanded the Silver Covenant and founded the Silversun Compact with Aethas Sunreaver, ending the feud between the high elves and the Sunreavers. Together they offered Anasterian's crown to help create the Dawnwell. She still refuses to see the Amani trolls as kin."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['umbric'] = [
  ["The Magister Who Studied the Void", [
    "Umbric studied magic in Dalaran with his best friend Rommath and the future traitor Dar'Khan Drathir, and the Void, the dark power of shadow, fascinated him from an early age. After the Scourge took Quel'Thalas he searched Dar'Khan's ruins for his research. He believed his people needed more than one source of power, since the Sunwell had failed them twice. When the Sunwell was restored, the Magisters banned Void rituals. Umbric defended his work in open council and would not give it up. Rommath stripped his title and talked the sentence down from death to exile.",
    "In the Ghostlands, Umbric used Dar'Khan's notes to reach Telogrus Rift, a broken world in the Void. There a Void ethereal began a ritual to unmake his followers. Alleria Windrunner stopped it, but the elves were changed for good. Umbric apologized for his arrogance, accepted Alleria as teacher and leader, and pledged his ren'dorei, the void elves, to the Alliance."
  ]],
  ["Return to Quel'Thalas", [
    "He fought for the Alliance in Zandalar and later on K'aresh. In Midnight he brought his people home to defend Quel'Thalas, faced Rommath again, and answered his fire with arcane magic instead of the Void. Umbric took a relic called the Cynosure of Twilight from the Magisters' Terrace to open the way into the Voidstorm. When Alleria was lost at the Voidspire, he pulled her son Arator back to Silvermoon. His Void crystal helped balance the corrupted well so it could become the Dawnwell. Rommath then gave the void elves an embassy in Silvermoon, and the old friends began working together again.",
    "Umbric now leads the void elves while Alleria is missing. He is proud and curious, and he takes the Void's dangers seriously. He has warned Arator that one seldom touches the Void only once."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['cenarius'] = [
  ["Lord of the Forest", [
    "Cenarius is a demigod of the forests, the son of the great White Stag Malorne. Night elf and tauren tradition name the moon goddess Elune as his mother, and the green dragon Ysera raised him in the Emerald Dream. His sons are the keepers of the grove and his daughters are the dryads. He befriended the first night elves and taught them about the natural world, then withdrew when they turned to the arcane.",
    "Ages later he found Malfurion Stormrage, saw great promise in him, and made him the first mortal druid. In the War of the Ancients he persuaded the Wild Gods to fight beside the night elves. When demons overwhelmed him, Malorne died saving him at the hands of Archimonde. Cenarius helped Malfurion imprison Illidan after the war, blessed the founding of the Cenarion Circle, and watched over the night elves' forests from Moonglade through the long ages that followed."
  ]],
  ["Death and Return", [
    "In the Third War, Cenarius sensed the Legion's taint in the newly arrived orcs and attacked Grom Hellscream's clan. The orcs drank the demon Mannoroth's blood and killed him. From the Emerald Dream his spirit watched orcs, humans and night elves defend the World Tree together at Hyjal, and he came to respect them. Later lore says he recovered in Ardenweald, the realm of the dead, before returning.",
    "During the Cataclysm he came back through the Dream to help defeat Ragnaros on Mount Hyjal. In Legion the Emerald Nightmare corrupted him in Val'sharah, and heroes freed him with Malfurion's help. He fought Fyrakk in the Emerald Dream during Dragonflight and attended the blooming of Amirdrassil. He remains a leader of the Cenarion Circle."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['shandris'] = [
  ["Tyrande's Daughter", [
    "Shandris Feathermoon lost her family when the Burning Legion destroyed her village in the War of the Ancients. Tyrande Whisperwind found the lonely girl among the refugees and took her in. Shandris followed Tyrande everywhere, slipped into battles against orders, and in the novels saved Malfurion's life with a well-placed arrow. She stayed at Tyrande's side through the Sundering and became a captain of the Sentinels.",
    "For ten thousand years she served her adoptive mother. She led the Shadowleaf Sentinels in the Third War and fought at Mount Hyjal. She rose to General of the Sentinels and commanded from Feathermoon Stronghold, nearly dying there when naga attacked after the Cataclysm. She fought at Theramore, joined the Unseen Path against the Legion, and led the night elf fleet in the War of the Thorns. In the Fourth War she co-led the Alliance campaign in Zandalar."
  ]],
  ["Leader of the Kaldorei", [
    "When the Night Warrior's power threatened Tyrande's life, Shandris followed her into the Maw and helped save her. After Amirdrassil bloomed, Tyrande and Malfurion named her leader of the night elves. In Midnight the blood elves, nightborne and void elves came to Bel'ameth for help. Over Maiev Shadowsong's objections, Shandris lent them her army without conditions and woke the ancients, though she urged Lor'themar to think hard before restoring the Sunwell's power. She joined the charge on Quel'Danas and offered Moonwell water to help cleanse the well. She is more open to peace with the Horde than her mother was, and she rules the night elves today."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['maiev'] = [
  ["The Warden", [
    "Maiev Shadowsong was a senior priestess of Elune and the older sister of Jarod Shadowsong, the commoner who led the night elf armies late in the War of the Ancients. When Malfurion chose to imprison his brother Illidan instead of executing him, Maiev thought it far too merciful. She volunteered to guard the prisoner, gathered priestesses into the Watchers, and took the title of Warden. For ten thousand years Illidan's prison was her life's work."
  ]],
  ["The Hunt and After", [
    "In the Third War, Tyrande freed Illidan and killed Watchers to do it. Maiev hunted him across Kalimdor to the Broken Isles, where he collapsed the Tomb of Sargeras on her soldiers. She lied to Malfurion that Tyrande was dead to sharpen his anger. She followed Illidan to Outland, captured him and lost him. Akama of the Broken led her into Illidan's ambush, and she spent years in a cell until Akama turned on his master and freed her. At the Black Temple she joined the heroes who killed Illidan. As he died he told her the huntress is nothing without the hunt, and she found it was true. She locked his body and his demon hunters in the Vault of the Wardens.",
    "Back home and without purpose, she was outraged when the Highborne were welcomed back. She secretly murdered several of them and tried to kill Malfurion, until her brother Jarod stopped her. In Legion she freed the demon hunters to fight the Legion and worked grudgingly beside a revived Illidan. After Teldrassil burned, Tyrande put her in command in Darkshore, and she led the night elves to retake it. In recent years she has softened toward change, even letting a male mage bear a protector's marks. In Midnight she opposed helping the blood elves with the Sunwell, then fought on Quel'Danas anyway. She remains Warden."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['xavius'] = [
  ["Counselor and First Satyr", [
    "Lord Xavius was Queen Azshara's high counselor and, since the queen hid her own power, the most respected sorcerer among the Highborne. He had replaced his eyes with magical orbs of black and ruby. Xavius was the first to hear Sargeras and believed a god had chosen him. He brought the voice to Azshara and led the Highborne in opening the portal in the Well of Eternity that let the Burning Legion into the world. Malfurion Stormrage, moving through the Emerald Dream, broke his spellwork and killed him.",
    "Sargeras tortured Xavius's spirit, then gave him a new body with hooves and horns. He became the first satyr and offered the same gift to other Highborne. Malfurion killed him again by trapping him in a tree. That tree survived the Sundering, and the Old Gods claimed him."
  ]],
  ["The Nightmare Lord", [
    "Ten thousand years later, Xavius served the Old God N'Zoth as the Nightmare Lord, spreading corruption through the Emerald Dream. He fed the grieving archdruid Fandral Staghelm false dreams of his dead son and had him graft a branch of Xavius's tree onto Teldrassil. He held Malfurion's spirit prisoner in the Dream for years. After the Lich King's fall, Malfurion broke free, and with many allies he destroyed Xavius once more. The satyr's spirit was sealed in the Rift of Aln.",
    "In Legion he returned in Val'sharah. He corrupted Cenarius, captured Malfurion and turned Ysera into a dragon of Nightmare. Heroes defeated him in the Rift of Aln, the heart of the Nightmare, and he turned back into a night elf as he fell. It is not certain that he is gone for good, but he has not returned since. He blamed Malfurion for every torment he suffered and pursued that grudge through every form he took."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['fandral'] = [
  ["The Hand of Earth", [
    "Fandral Staghelm was a night elf druid with a hot temper and a fierce love for his only son, Valstann. Some 4,500 years before the First War, he planted branches of Nordrassil across the world without the Cenarion Circle's approval. One of them, Andrassil in Northrend, sank its roots into the prison of the Old God Yogg-Saron and had to be destroyed, and through it the corruption that became the Emerald Nightmare entered the Dream.",
    "About a thousand years before the First War, Fandral led the night elves against the insect armies of the qiraji in the War of the Shifting Sands. He fought so well that the qiraji named him Hand of Earth. Their general Rajaxx lured Valstann into a trap and killed him in front of his father. Fandral lost the will to fight, and he never recovered from the loss."
  ]],
  ["The Fallen Archdruid", [
    "After the Third War, with Malfurion lost in the Dream, Fandral became Archdruid and persuaded the druids to plant a new World Tree, Teldrassil, without the dragons' blessing. The Nightmare Lord Xavius had promised him in dreams that his son could live again. Fandral grafted a branch of Xavius's tree onto Teldrassil and used a grain called morrowgrain to keep Malfurion's sleeping body weak. When Malfurion returned and destroyed the false Valstann, Fandral collapsed into near catatonia and was imprisoned on Mount Hyjal.",
    "During the Cataclysm a green dragon secretly serving Ragnaros handed him to the Firelord's allies. Fandral founded the Druids of the Flame, became Ragnaros's majordomo, and shattered Thrall's spirit in an ambush. Heroes killed him in the Firelands."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['elune'] = [
  ["The Mother Moon", [
    "Elune is a moon goddess and the center of night elf faith. She is tied to the larger of Azeroth's two moons, the White Lady. The first night elves believed she slept in the Well of Eternity by day. Her priestesses, the Sisters of Elune, heal the wounded and call down her moonlight on enemies, and moonwells are pools whose water she has blessed. Tradition names her the mother of Cenarius by the White Stag Malorne. The tauren honor her as Mu'sha, the Moon, one of the Earth Mother's eyes.",
    "Elune has never been seen in physical form on Azeroth. She speaks to her high priestess through visions and has shielded and healed her chosen. Her gentler side, the Mother Moon, values peace and harmony. Another aspect, the Night Warrior, carries her fury. Tyrande Whisperwind took on that power after Teldrassil burned."
  ]],
  ["What Is Known and What Is Not", [
    "The Shadowlands revealed more of her. Elune is a power of Life and the sister of the Winter Queen, who rules Ardenweald in the realm of Death. She sent the souls of the night elves who died at Teldrassil to help her starving sister, not knowing they would fall into the Maw. When she learned the truth, she spoke through Tyrande and shed a single tear. With the Winter Queen it became the Sisters' Tear, the seed that grew into Amirdrassil.",
    "Much about her remains a mystery. Her true origin is unknown. Velen once suggested she might be a naaru, and Khadgar has wondered whether she created the first naaru, but neither idea has been confirmed. Xal'atath calls her an upstart and a great enemy, for reasons no one has explained. Night elves themselves admit they may never understand the ways of the gods."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['thalyssra'] = [
  ["First Arcanist", [
    "Thalyssra was a Highborne mage of Suramar before the War of the Ancients and lived through ten thousand years under the city's shield, slowly changing with her people into the nightborne. She rose to First Arcanist, a close adviser to Grand Magistrix Elisande. When Gul'dan demanded that Suramar join the Legion, she argued that the demons would not bargain if they could break the shield themselves. She led a small coup to stop the shield from falling. Her ally Melandrus stabbed her in the back and threw her into the river."
  ]],
  ["Leader of the Nightfallen", [
    "Thalyssra survived as a starving exile and sent a hidden call for help that Khadgar answered. From the refuge of Shal'Aran she built the Nightfallen rebellion, and her allies grew an arcan'dor tree whose fruit cured her need for the Nightwell. With Tyrande's night elves, Liadrin's blood elves and Vereesa's high elves, the rebels stormed the Nighthold, and Elisande fell. Thalyssra took power and let the dying Nightwell fade, so her people would never again depend on it.",
    "Tyrande's distrust and Liadrin's sympathy led her to the Horde, and after visiting Silvermoon she pledged Suramar to it. In the Fourth War she joined the rebellion against Sylvanas and took a seat on the Horde Council. She and Lor'themar Theron grew close over a poetry contest and later married in Suramar.",
    "In Midnight she joined the envoy to the night elves. When her own army failed to arrive, she found that the Twilight's Blade had killed her steward, taken his place and drained the city's mana. Once that was stopped, her forces supported the attack on Quel'Danas from the sea, and she gave a fruit of the arcan'dor to help cleanse the Darkwell. She remains First Arcanist."
  ]]
];

window.CHAR_BIOS['elisande'] = [
  ["Guardian of Suramar", [
    "Elisande led a circle of Highborne in Suramar who were loyal to Queen Azshara and gathered powerful relics, including the titans' Pillars of Creation. She grew to distrust the Legion, and when she learned that demons planned a second portal in Suramar, she used the Pillars to seal it. She did not join the march on Zin-Azshari and stayed to protect her city. With the Eye of Aman'Thul she created the Nightwell and raised a shield that saved most of Suramar from the Great Sundering. For ten thousand years she ruled under that dome as Grand Magistrix, while the Nightwell turned her people into the nightborne. She became a master of time magic."
  ]],
  ["The Price of Survival", [
    "When Gul'dan came with the Legion's offer, Elisande looked into possible futures and saw only ruin if Suramar resisted. She lowered the shield and gave the demons the Nightwell. As Thalyssra's rebellion grew, she grew crueler. She rationed the arcwine her people depended on, ordered the massacre of the Waning Crescent district, and sent citizens to the Legion to be killed. At the Nighthold's gates she froze the combined elven army in time. Rebels broke in through the depths, and Elisande died atop the Nightspire, still insisting she had chosen her people's survival.",
    "An echo of her, shaken by a defeat she had not foreseen, renounced the Legion and lent the heroes power over time in their fight with Gul'dan. Elisande was proud and coldly practical. Like Kael'thas before her, she believed serving the Legion would save her people, and it cost her everything."
  ]]
];
